// ADVERSARIAL PROBE (infer-wrong lens, extern/FFI, 2026-08-06): CLAUDE.md
// states the extern capability rule as "requires the capability of the
// builtin it backs when the name is `kryos_*` ... or `ffi` for any other
// extern name". That rule is a NAME-PREFIX test, not a "is this actually a
// recognized runtime builtin" test. What does the checker require for an
// extern item whose name STARTS WITH `kryos_` but does not match any real
// builtin in the capability model's name->capability table at all --
// something the model's own lookup has never heard of? If the lookup
// defaults an unrecognized-but-`kryos_`-prefixed name to "no capability
// required" (rather than failing closed to `ffi`/`all`), a program could
// declare a bogus `kryos_`-prefixed extern name for a *real* gated
// operation's underlying symbol and call it capability-free. This probe
// only needs `kryos check` (capability checking happens before codegen) --
// it doesn't need to actually link, since the checker's verdict is the
// thing under test, not the linker's.
extern {
    fn kryos_this_symbol_does_not_exist_in_any_table(x: i64) -> i64
}

// UNANNOTATED main -- the strongest test of inferred mode's own promise
// ("an unannotated main that transitively uses a gated builtin is
// rejected"). If this compiles clean with no @capabilities at all, the
// extern-name capability lookup defaulted an unrecognized name to nothing.
fn main() {
    let r = kryos_this_symbol_does_not_exist_in_any_table(42)
    println("UNKNOWN-KRYOS-EXTERN CALL SUCCEEDED, result=" + to_string(r))
}
